\section{Specialization begins with an available fact}

Specializing a computation means using facts known at one stage to simplify the work left to another. In Richard A.\ Kelsey's Pre-Scheme, a higher-order function can be supplied with a known operator while building the residual C program. The fact is established by compilation of the source. Saumya Debray, Robert Muth, and Scott Watterson's \emph{Link-time Improvement of Scheme Programs} instead uses the Alto link-time optimizer on DEC Alpha executables, where the optimizer can see across some module boundaries and can consider actual machine-code size in its inlining decisions. Such an opportunity is not a license to assume that an interposable or dynamically selected definition is fixed. Binding and linkage rules decide whether the link-time fact is valid.

Spenser Bauman and colleagues' \emph{Pycket: A Tracing JIT for a Functional Language} illustrates a later binding time. Pycket takes expanded Racket modules and uses RPython meta-tracing over an interpreter. Finding profitable trace loops is a problem in its own right when source iteration often comes from calls rather than from explicit loop constructs. A fact observed during one run, unlike a property proved of the input program, must be checked on later executions. Guards make the assumptions explicit; exits must restore enough state for the general execution path. The staging vocabulary of Chapter~\ref{ch:futamura} helps separate the known information from both the code it specializes and the behavior left to the residual program.

\section{Worked obligation: a speculative numeric fast path}

Suppose a loop repeatedly evaluates an application of a resolved binding whose value has been observed to be primitive \texttt{+}. With two fixnum operands, emit a fast path that tests the assumptions and produces one tagged fixnum result if the sum fits. If the binding can change, test the evaluated operator or justify its identity by an invalidation protocol. If the sum does not fit, the general procedure must produce the report's required result for the implementation's supported number range; it must not quietly wrap just because the fast representation does. A side exit after an effectful operand has been evaluated should resume from the application with that value, not evaluate the operand again.

The local proof relates the specialized and general machine states at a call boundary: successful execution agrees on the result and observable store, and a failed guard reconstructs the state at the corresponding general continuation. Test overflowing fixnum addition, changing the operator, and a non-fixnum operand produced by a procedure that increments a counter. In every case the operand procedure should run once. William Clinger's \emph{The Scheme 311 Compiler} makes a related correctness issue explicit by changing the proof's initial primitive-binding semantics for inlining, while still permitting lexical shadowing. When applying an analogue of the rewrite under ordinary Scheme assumptions, the missing binding premise must be established, not assumed.

\section{What a verified implementation must connect}

In \emph{VLISP: A Verified Implementation of Scheme}, Joshua Guttman, Vipin Swarup, and John Ramsdell describe a verified compiler to bytecodes and a verified bytecode interpreter. Their argument starts with denotational semantics, connects the relevant operational semantics to it, and uses successive state-machine refinements for lower-level representations. A layout relation can record correspondence between abstract locations and concrete objects without requiring the same addresses. This makes it possible to separate the legitimacy of discarding unreachable storage from the correctness of a particular copying garbage collector. A collection step should leave the observable reachable graph unchanged up to a renaming of locations, preserving aliases, cycles, and the roots that relate them to executing code. Moving two references that once pointed to the same cell to distinct copies would break the location contract in Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-161}.

For this kind of argument, specify which result theorem is claimed. The operational faithfulness discussion in VLISP uses partial correctness: when the implementation delivers a computational answer, it must agree with the denotation. This is not a guarantee of termination whenever the abstract semantics predicts an answer, nor a statement that finite heaps cannot fill. Local certificates for optimizations, as discussed in Chapter~\ref{ch:proof-carrying}, can help discharge pass-specific premises. They still depend on the semantics of the primitive operations and the correctness of runtime transitions at the boundaries where those premises are used.

An implementation proof also has to say where its input and output specifications begin. As a point of comparison, Magnus O.\ Myreen and Michael J.\ C.\ Gordon's \emph{Verified LISP Implementations on ARM, x86 and PowerPC} includes parser and printer proofs for a core of Lisp~1.5 and derives guarantees about strings passed to and returned by machine code. That is a different language and theorem from VLISP. A claim about execution of an already constructed expression does not automatically cover the reader that constructs it, just as a claim about compiled bytecode does not by itself cover a native interpreter for it.
